 and display one thing while  it's running and then another thing  while it's completed after it's  completed so here we have another  example that gets into a wait state with  a little animated ellipsis again and  then finally when it's done goes to the  state where everything's warmed up so  this happens more often than you might  think I thought I'd pointed out as a  common difficulty that people have and  we've made this especially complicated  for users of dynamic module by taking  care of the problem entirely for  manipulate so we've hidden this problem  manipulate by having our own custom wait  cursor that we put up automatically  whenever there's an asynchronous  initialization maybe you've seen it in  the past but that's why you haven't if  you're if you're strictly a manipulate  user and you're itching to get your  fingers into dynamic module this is one  thing you'll need to be aware of okay  again bonus slides I won't be opening  this is for you and for the rest of the  talk I'm going to sort of outline some  common mistakes and pitfalls that people  encounter when using dynamic module one  that comes up fairly often is they think  just because they put a symbol into the  initialization option that it is  therefore localized by the dynamic  module that is not the case like module  the only variables that are local are  the ones that you list and the first  argument to the dynamic module you need  to specify which variables are local it  doesn't automatically sort of figure out  which variables you want to be local so  what does this mean this means that you  can drop an initialization setting into  a dynamic module that sets a global  function say to sum through something  and then you have another dynamic module  saying that sets that same global  variable to something else  and notice that out 26 here is Duc when  I evaluate this next guy this now has  changed to rabbit because this season  and this season are the same they are  referring to the same global function so  how do you how do you tell dynamic  module that you want to localize the  utility well you can tell it by  including the name of the utility in the  first argument because that's where you  say where you want things localized and  this is a perfectly reasonable way and  good way to localize things there's  another way that that I will mention  because it's used in the demonstration  site and it's used by our help viewer I  will point out the  presence of an option called cell  context which sets up a default context  in which dynamic evaluations occur or  any evaluation occurs someone could  imagine and I haven't imagined it I've  actually done it  taking the dynamic modules with the  unload season so this is global in some  sense in this expression cell but I've  told this expression cell use a unique  context that's unique to this cell  automatically so even so the context of  season is not global when in this output  the context of season is cell dollar  four seven five or something so in this  way one could imagine having you know  different rectangles on screen  corresponding to different ambient  contexts and localizing things in that  way rather than having them be local to  the dynamic module well question mark  season is going to tell us the  definition from above these guys are in  a different context so I think this is  the interesting question here so here  are the here are the automated contexts  that the front end has created for for  these instances of that symbol yes is  the question that can I set this to a  string is that okay so so the question  is can I set cell context to a string  rather than cell and yes in fact the  global default of cell context is in  fact global but it could be you know my  context 3 or whatever you like note that  that would give it a string it needs to  be a valid context name so it ends in  this back tick okay if if you added that  setting that same setting to the other  cell as well yes this this option can  also be set at the notebook level so you  can have a notebook and this is what's  done for demonstrations for instance and  for our health viewers so that's nice  okay important thing to talk about is is  this red X which we saw earlier in the  in the talk why is it that we say that  this is a likely syntax error  or we're flagging it for special  consideration by giving it this  beautiful red color well remember module  names are temporary they exist only in  the kernel and when we try to provide  them as the basis for a dynamic which  wants a persistent reference to some  variable to key off of those two things  are inconsistent with one another at  their core and that's why the rule of  thumb is that dynamic should never  depend on local module variables and  I'll spell out exactly what that means  with a very nice example that I found on  on this website I think this was  actually from John Foltz gave a nice  sort of summary so when is it okay to  use module and dynamic together well  it's okay if the dynamic completely  scopes your usage of module so the  module is completely contained within  the dynamic it's OK for module to create  a dynamic as long as the dynamic it  creates doesn't refer to any local  module variables same for manipulate you  can create and manipulate you can spit a  manipulate out of a module as long as it  doesn't refer to local module variables  remember these local module variables  are the temporary ones that are going to  be automatically deleted so but these  are cases where you have a dynamic or a  manipulate which implies dynamic that  refers to a local module variable and  that's just no good it's not going to  work it's going to fail for a number of  reasons the question is are there  similar issues with width and width is  is a completely different animal  I would not imagine there to be scoping  conflicts in this way with width we can  we can look at those together perhaps  but right so so how do you avoid using  module and dynamic together don't use  them together you  dynamic module so and the syntax  coloring hopefully is there to help you  remember that okay let's see another  common mistake that I see more often  than I thought I'd see is a fundamental  misunderstanding of lexical scoping so  this comes up most often in manipulate  where somebody has a function that  refers to a couple of variables that are  kind of ambient they're not passed into  the function and then they say  manipulate of my function and they want  to manipulate those variables and they  say well why is my plot empty and these  are not stupid people these are smart  people but they haven't I guess we've we  have a failure of education here here's  the same problem with dynamic module  directly an empty plot why is this well  the variables outside in your definition  of F are in a different scope than the  ones in dynamic module this by the way  happens for module as well so if people  had been thinking clearly about dynamic  module as a scoping construct as a  lexical scoping construct perhaps they  would have gotten to the point where  they think Oh  lexical these things need to be in the  scope of the module in order for them to  work so how do you fix that well the  simplest thing is just to pass the  variables in this case by a value to the  utility so rather than just having f of  X and having a and B kind of be  magically passed you pass them through  directly okay I think we have time for  one more common problem in the case of  building windowed interfaces especially  for interfaces that I've built for our  internal use I find more and more the  need to handle cases where the kernel  quits in an interesting way so we have  an interface that where the user has  built up some state we don't want to  really do that state automatically after  the kernel quits because maybe they have  changed their kernel maybe they've  switched to something else but we want  to let them know that the kernel has  qu